p('<b>A conditional WRRA computation and a precise limit on address-color coupling</b>')
p('Wonsik Choi and Jeongin Choi<br/>Independent Research, Seoul, Republic of Korea<br/>20 October 2026 | Research note v0.5<br/>Release: WRRA-COLOR-AUDIT-20261020-v0.5<br/>Correspondence: janefather@gmail.com | ORCID 0009-0001-4263-9772')
h('Abstract')
p(f'We audit the particle/component branch of Minimal Computing Cosmology 3.0, using frozen source files and an unchanged finite-address calibration. A targeted rerun reproduces 427,892 odd-composite addresses, phenotype weight 0.050000000000000065, the reported conditional component fractions and an assembly-order total-variation distance of 0.20472100116369085. The declared antisymmetric three-color state is annihilated by all eight total color generators. Independent tests verify generator closure, collective SU(3) invariance, an exact one-dimensional common kernel and detection of symmetry-breaking controls. The declared replay, algebra, control and provenance checks pass; these overlapping tests are not independent observational evidence. Source inspection also establishes that the original color test accepts no arithmetic-state argument: three colors and their generators are explicitly supplied. We prove the consequent interface restriction: within three fundamental color factors and the exact total-color-singlet constraint, any joint address-color density operator factors as an address density operator times the fixed singlet projector. Thus this color sector cannot by itself encode address-dependent distinctions. The contribution is an executable WRRA interface audit and a conditional restriction on future coupling rules, rather than an arithmetic derivation of SU(3), confinement or the particle spectrum.')
h('Assessment contract')
p('<b>Verification inputs:</b> frozen finite-address parameters, adopted spectral inputs, inherited component decoder and charge table, and an explicitly chosen three-dimensional fundamental color representation.')
p('<b>WRRA transformation:</b> phase-aware admission weights feed the cyclic prime-component assembly; the inherited charge and color contracts are checked; an independent kernel audit determines what address information the exact color sector can retain.')
p('<b>Outputs:</b> component replay, charge neutrality, color-invariance residuals, exact singlet uniqueness and the factorization restriction.')
p('<b>Falsification conditions:</b> failure of the fixed-input replay, normalization or integer conservation; nonzero total-color action on the proposed singlet; a second independent common-kernel vector; or an admissible joint singlet-sector state that does not factor as proved below. Physical application requires a specified additional mapping to observations.')
story.append(PageBreak())
h('1. Frozen inputs and original computation')
p('WRRA abbreviates Worldline-Residue-Resource-Action. In this note it identifies a finite-state construction in which an address measure is filtered into sectors before a component interpretation is tested. The audited original Stage-3 claim is narrowly that the finite-source replay preserves assembly conservation, charge-neutral motifs and an inherited color singlet. The original README already labels the particle decoder conditional and spin and binding open. This audit does not attribute a stronger arithmetic derivation claim to that source.')
p('The inspected repository is Wonsik-Choi-janefather/wrra-m-0.1 at commit 9d9daf21154e46e55b9fd0fd461c7c070156ab94. The Stage-2 handoff fixes N=1,015,000, alpha=1.8996877935161325 and h=1.4476744689338703. Address 1 is inactive. The address weights are proportional to n^(-alpha). Four adopted zeta-zero heights and eight admission frames determine the original odd-composite admission effect. These are disclosed construction inputs; no parameter is refitted in this audit.')
eq('p(n) = w(n) e_phi(n) / sum_m w(m) e_phi(m)')
p('For odd composites, e_phi(n)=1-product_k[1-sigmoid(d_k(n)-h)], k=0,...,7, where d_k(n)=sum_j cos[gamma_j(log n+0.1k)]/2 and gamma=(14.134725141734695,21.022039638771556,25.01085758014569,30.424876125859512). Other addresses have zero phenotype effect. For each K-prime pool, begin with floor(n/sum(primes)) copies of every prime. Repeatedly traverse the selected order, taking one prime whenever the remaining residue permits it, until the residue is below 2. This preserves n=sum_j count_j prime_j+residue. The displayed candidate fraction is the p-weighted count of components labeled 5 or 47 divided by the p-weighted total component count, rather than a fraction of addresses.')
p('Here sigmoid(x)=1/(1+exp(-x)). For a given order o, define a_j(o)=sum_n p(n) count_j(n,o), and the number-share distribution P_j(o)=a_j(o)/sum_l a_l(o), aligning components by ascending prime label. The reported K15 distance is TV=(1/2)sum_j |P_j(small)-P_j(large)|. It compares these component-number distributions, not address probabilities or mass-weighted abundances.')
p('The original assembly function is replayed with the first 15 and first 16 prime labels in ascending and descending order. The labels {5,47} used to form a neutron candidate are historically fitted inputs. Their outputs are conditional component statistics, not newly derived neutron identities. The audit executes the original address, admission, assembly, charge and color function bodies extracted by Python AST. It bypasses unrelated import-time macro modules; it is a targeted rerun, not the full original Stage-3 pipeline.')
table([['Branch','Conditional neutron fraction','Mean component count'],*[ [k,format(v['neutron_fraction_5_47'],'.12f'),format(v['mean_parts'],'.10f')] for k,v in r['profiles'].items()]],[125,175,175])
p('The K15 ascending/descending number-share distributions differ by total-variation distance 0.20472100116369085. This preserves a real order dependence in the declared assembly. A unique particle decoder does not follow from those rules. All four assemblies preserve the exact integer address reconstruction and normalized component shares. The inherited neutral charge motifs remain exactly neutral using rational arithmetic.')
p('The charge table supplies 16 channels, including up-type charges +2/3, down-type -1/3 and electron -1. The tested motifs are uud+electron, udd, an electron/conjugate pair, and an individually neutral channel. Charges are summed as exact rational numbers. Charge assignments and particle names are supplied inputs in this test.')
