h('2. What the original color function supplies')
p('The original color_check() has no parameters. It creates a 3 x 3 x 3 complex tensor, assigns the six signed permutations explicitly, constructs six off-diagonal and two diagonal color matrices, and tests their total action. It never reads N, alpha, h, admission weights, assembly counts or neutron labels. Consequently the original replay establishes compatibility with its supplied color contract. It does not establish that arithmetic selected three colors or the SU(3) algebra.')
eq('|S> = (|rgb> + |gbr> + |brg>\n- |rbg> - |grb> - |bgr>) / sqrt(6)')
p('For standard normalized fundamental generators T_a with Tr(T_a T_b)=delta_ab/2, let Q_a=T_a tensor I tensor I + I tensor T_a tensor I + I tensor I tensor T_a. The original function uses matrices twice this normalization; annihilation and the common kernel are unchanged.')
p('The eight-matrix fundamental SU(3) basis and the epsilon contraction defining the baryon color singlet are established representation-theory background; see David Tong, The Standard Model, chapter 3, The Strong Force. They are not claimed as new mathematics. The new work here is the source-dependency inspection, the pinned targeted replay and explicit application of the rank-one restriction to this WRRA interface. Collective rotation invariance also follows directly from the determinant-one identity U tensor U tensor U acting on the epsilon tensor.')
p('The intended readers are implementers of the WRRA particle/component interface. The audit changes a concrete development decision: passing the current stand-alone singlet test cannot justify deleting address-dependent structure or calling the color group an arithmetic output. An implementer must instead specify and test a coupling, or retain the color sector as an adopted contract. This is model-specific implementation knowledge; its value does not depend on claiming new SU(3) representation theory.')
p('Throughout this note, independent construction means a separately implemented matrix calculation within the same audit, rather than independent authorship or external experimental replication. No outside laboratory validation is asserted.')
p('dependency_audit.py additionally verifies the frozen source hash, recursively inspects external reads in the compiled function and nested comprehensions, and executes the extracted function with only NumPy, itertools, math and five required builtins. The complete external-name set is {complex, float, itertools, math, max, np, range, sum}; there is no captured closure state or arithmetic global read. All four dependency checks pass. This assumes ordinary unmodified installed libraries; it is not a sandbox against malicious module replacement. These additional provenance checks are separate from the original 36-check audit.')
