h('3. Conditional restriction on address-color coupling')
p('<b>Proposition.</b> Let H_A be any finite address Hilbert space, H_C=(C^3) tensor (C^3) tensor (C^3), and let the three factors carry the same fundamental SU(3) action. Suppose a joint density operator Omega is supported on the exact zero-total-color subspace: Omega=(I_A tensor P) Omega (I_A tensor P), where P projects onto the common kernel of all eight Q_a. Then P=|S><S| and there is a unique address density operator sigma_A such that')
eq('Omega = sigma_A tensor |S><S|.')
p('<b>Proof of the common kernel.</b> In the color basis, the two diagonal traceless generators require equal occupation of r, g and b in each surviving three-factor basis tensor. Only the six permutations of rgb can remain. For each pair of colors i,j, the off-diagonal raising operator E_ij acts on the two permutations that differ by exchanging those colors in their tensor positions, producing the same repeated-color output. Its vanishing requires their coefficients to have opposite signs. These transposition relations connect all six permutations, so all coefficients are fixed by one amplitude and permutation parity. Normalization gives |S>. Direct substitution verifies every Q_a|S>=0. Thus the kernel is one-dimensional.')
p('<b>Proof of factorization.</b> Expand Omega in address matrix units: Omega=sum_nm |n><m| tensor C_nm. Support on the rank-one color projector implies C_nm=P C_nm P=c_nm P. Therefore Omega=sigma_A tensor P, with sigma_A=[c_nm]. Positivity and unit trace of Omega give positivity and unit trace of sigma_A; partial trace over color proves uniqueness. This is a finite linear-algebra consequence of the declared representation and constraint, not a new general theorem about QCD.')
h('4. Implication for the WRRA interface')
p('For any address observable B and color observable C, the expectation becomes Tr(sigma_A B) <S|C|S>. An observable acting only on this color sector cannot distinguish different address distributions. More strongly, a controlled transformation U=sum_n |n><n| tensor (U_n tensor U_n tensor U_n), with every U_n in SU(3), acts as the identity on the exact singlet-supported sector. Such collective color rotations cannot transfer address distinctions into this sector.')
p('<b>Concrete implementation decision.</b> Consider two address preparations sigma_A=|a><a| and sigma_A=|b><b|, a different from b, each tensored with the exact singlet projector. A proposed decoder that deletes the address register and recovers the two different component labels from a color-only observable cannot work: both reduced color states are exactly |S><S|, so every such query has the same answer. Address-controlled collective SU(3) rotations do not repair the loss because each leaves the singlet invariant. Therefore an implementation must retain the address/component register when those labels are requested; passing the standalone color test is insufficient grounds to discard it. This is an example under the existing contract, not a newly proposed particle mechanism.')
p('This result narrows the implementation task. To make an arithmetic update change a color-sensitive output, an additional executable coupling or larger state space is needed. Possible choices include multiple singlet copies in a larger representation, retained spatial or flavor factors, or explicitly tracked nonsinglet intermediate states with a declared admissibility rule. This note selects none of these possibilities as physical and claims no completion of spin, flavor, gauge fields, binding or confinement.')
p('The restriction concerns exact singlet support. It does not follow merely from zero mean color charge, which is weaker. It also does not apply unchanged to a color subsystem entangled with gluons or other omitted factors. These distinctions are part of the stated scope.')
